## Founding & History
Mewstone Wines was established in 2011 by brothers Matthew and Jonathan (Jonny) Hughes. The vineyard is located on the banks of the D'Entrecasteaux Channel in Flowerpot, southern Tasmania. The property was formerly a cherry orchard. Matthew Hughes recounts that they were inspired to purchase the property after tasting a now-defunct wine label from a nearby vineyard in the same subregion. The brothers spotted the land for sale in 2010.

The first small crop from their plantings was in 2015, from which Jonathan made a Pinot Noir that was not released. The first vintage under the Mewstone Wines label was officially released in 2016. The name "Mewstone" was inspired by a large rock Matthew's wife saw on the East Coast of Tasmania, and a story about French sailors taking a wrong bearing off Mewstone.

## People
Mewstone Wines is owned and operated by brothers Matthew and Jonathan Hughes. Jonathan (Jonny) Hughes is the principal winemaker. Matthew Hughes co-owns and manages the winery. Jonny Hughes gained winemaking experience across Australia, New Zealand, Italy, and Canada. He spent seven years as Assistant Winemaker at Moorilla in Hobart before establishing Mewstone. Matthew handles other aspects of the business, with assistance from vineyard manager Luke Andree. Daniel Way joined the team as vineyard manager in 2025.

## Vineyard & Site
Mewstone Vineyard is located in Flowerpot, on the banks of the D'Entrecasteaux Channel in southern Tasmania. The vineyard was established on a former cherry orchard. It is nearing its final size of 5.2 hectares. Other sources state the vineyard expanded to 3.6 hectares with new plantings in 2017, or 3.5 hectares with new plantings in 2019. As of 2025, it is reported to be 5.3 hectares under vine.

The vineyard sits approximately 100 meters from the D'Entrecasteaux Channel. Its proximity to the water moderates the temperature and extends the growing season, promoting slow, even ripening and fruit purity. The site receives reflective UV from the water, making it a bright but cool location. The slopes are gently north-facing, capturing cool southern sunshine, and the vineyard is protected from harsh westerly winds by surrounding hills.

The original plantings in 2011 included Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, Syrah, and Riesling. After trialling several varieties, the focus has been refined to Pinot Noir, Chardonnay, and Riesling, which are consistently planted. Pinot Noir and Chardonnay form the backbone of the vineyard. In 2017-18, additional clones of Pinot Noir and Riesling were planted, with further plantings in 2023 and 2024. Specific Pinot Noir clones added in 2018 include Abel, ENTAV-INRA 828, 943, and 667.

The soils are predominantly duplex soils formed on Jurassic dolerite, consisting of loam-textured topsoil over a sandy loam horizon on clay subsoil. The farming approach prioritizes vineyard health, with a focus on healthy soil, vines, and fruit. The vineyard is hand pruned and the fruit is hand-picked. They use mulches, composts, and compost teas to improve soils. When the team arrived in 2011, a soil ecologist found very little fungi, with a bacteria-to-fungi ratio of 10:1.

## Cellar Door Experience
Mewstone Wines' cellar door is open Friday to Monday from 11 am to 4 pm. Bookings are preferred to ensure the intimate tasting space is not crowded. Guided tastings cost $25 per person and include wines from both the Mewstone and Hughes & Hughes ranges. The cellar door is described as having giant glass doors and high design. It is situated in Flowerpot, approximately a 40-minute drive south of Hobart along the D'Entrecasteaux Channel. The location offers views of Bruny Island and the Wellington Ranges.

## Food & Hospitality
Locally sourced seasonal platters are available at the cellar door. The cellar door can also be hired exclusively for private experiences and can cater for small groups under 50 people. Owners Matt and Jonathan can conduct private vineyard tours and tastings for groups.

## Awards & Recognition
Mewstone Wines has received several awards and recognitions:
* Best New Winery of the Year – James Halliday's Wine Companion, August 2018.
* Trophy, Best New Act: Jonathan Hughes – Young Gun of Wine, June 2018.
* Young Winemaker of the Year – 2019.
* Best Tasting Experience.
* Top Wineries Australia – 2021 and 2022.
* Jonny Hughes was a Young Gun of Wine Finalist in 2020, 2021, 2023, and 2025.
* Mewstone was awarded New Vineyard of the Year in 2021 by Young Gun of Wine.
* Mewstone Wines Hughes & Hughes Riesling 2024 Tasmania received a 92 silver rating.
* Mewstone Wines Hughes & Hughes Riesling 2025 Tasmania received a 94 silver rating.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Winemaker Jonny Hughes prioritizes the health of the vineyard, focusing on healthy soil, vines, and fruit. They have never inoculated or fined their wines. They use extensive mulches, composts, and compost teas to continually improve their soils. The vineyard is hand pruned and the fruit is hand-picked. The Hughes & Hughes wines are bottled unfined and with low sulfur.

## Visiting Practical Info
Address: 11 Flowerpot Jetty Road, Flowerpot, Tas 7162.
Phone: (03) 6155 4416.
Website: www.mewstonewines.com.au.
The winery is located approximately 40-45 minutes drive south of Hobart. Bookings are preferred for cellar door visits.

## Notable / Distinctive
Mewstone Wines is a family-owned winery established by brothers Matthew and Jonathan Hughes in the D'Entrecasteaux Channel subregion of Tasmania. The vineyard was once a cherry orchard. The site's proximity to the D'Entrecasteaux Channel, which acts like a lake due to the protection of Bruny Island, provides unique climatic conditions with early sea breezes and reflective UV, contributing to a longer ripening season and distinct Pinot Noir characteristics. Their Pinot Noir is noted for significant tannin levels and high skin-to-juice ratio due to small berries, achieving flavor ripeness at low alcohol levels.

The winery operates two labels: Mewstone, focusing on estate-grown, minimal intervention wines, and Hughes & Hughes, which explores multi-regional Tasmanian fruit and experimental, lo-fi winemaking techniques, sometimes without sulfur additions. The cellar door, designed by Preston Lane architects, is noted for its high design and natural light.

